// Presets, offered as rows of buttons at the top of the config screen and by /dsconfig preset, in this order (see
// lib/config/ConfigPreset and config/PresetLoader): each "group" is a row, "features" (the default) and "performance".
// Modpacks add their own in config/dsurround/configs/<namespace>/presets.json or a resource pack's
// assets/<namespace>/dsconfigs/presets.json: a new id is added after these, the same id replaces one of these, and
// {"id": "rpg", "hidden": true} removes one. Optional "name" and "description" show instead of the translations; a
// new group needs a "dsurround.config.presets.group.<group>" translation, or shows its name.
// Presets add up: each sets only the properties it lists, so Vanilla+ then RPG gives RPG feedback in an otherwise
// plain world. The properties fall into four themes, a preset to turn each on:
//   Combat & RPG (RPG): pop-offs, crit words, swing/bow/jump/toolbar sounds, burning flames, death mist, footprints,
//     skull flames
//   Weather & sky (Atmosphere): storms, particle precipitation, dust, drips, window streaks and frost, ground fog,
//     clouds, fog, auroras, wind on particles, rain ripples, storm and thunder sounds
//   World details (World): steam, bubble columns, flame jets, waterfalls, fireflies, breath
//   Sound polish: ambience, remapped steps, footstep accents, brush steps, waterfall sounds
// Left out on purpose: the HUD (compass, clock, light levels: personal layout), Scary Content (a comfort choice no
// preset turns back on), ranges and quality (the performance presets'), technical settings, and anything that needs
// a restart (the music manager).
// The performance presets (Low, Medium, High) set quality and ranges, and the costliest details measured with the
// bench (scripts/bench): waterfall mist and froth, dynamic clouds, and the light from flames and fireflies. Those
// three are also features, so a feature preset applied after Low turns them back on: accepted.
// A test checks every path names a property and every value fits it.
